.\" xargs(1) - substrate
.TH XARGS 1 "2026-06-02" "Substrate" "General Commands Manual"
.SH NAME
xargs \- construct argument lists and invoke a utility
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xargs
.RB [ \-0oprtx ]
.RB [ \-E
.IR eofstr ]
.RB [ \-e [\fIeofstr\fR]]
.RB [ \-I
.IR replstr ]
.RB [ \-J
.IR replstr ]
.RB [ \-L
.IR number ]
.RB [ \-l [\fInumber\fR]]
.RB [ \-n
.IR number ]
.RB [ \-P
.IR maxprocs ]
.RB [ \-R
.IR replcount ]
.RB [ \-S
.IR replsize ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR size ]
.RB [ \-a
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR delim ]
.RI [ utility
.RI [ argument ...]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xargs
reads space, tab, newline, and end-of-file delimited items from the standard
input (or from a file given with
.BR \-a )
and executes
.I utility
with the items as additional arguments.  Blank, tab and newline characters
separate items; single quotes, double quotes and backslash may be used to
embed separators in an item.  If no
.I utility
is given,
.B echo
is used.  The utility is run at least once even when the input is empty,
unless
.B \-r
is specified.
.PP
This implementation conforms to POSIX.1\-2024 and additionally provides the
GNU and BSD extensions; where the standards conflict the BSD behavior is
chosen.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-0 ", " \-\-null
Items are separated by a NUL character instead of whitespace, and quoting,
backslash escaping and the logical EOF string are disabled.  Pairs with
.IR find (1)
.BR \-print0 .
.TP
.BI \-a " file" "\fR, \fP" \-\-arg\-file= file
Read items from
.I file
rather than the standard input.  May be repeated.
.TP
.BI \-d " delim" "\fR, \fP" \-\-delimiter= delim
Use
.I delim
as the sole item separator (a single character or a C escape such as
.BR \en ", " \et ", " \e0 ", " \exHH );
disables quoting and backslash processing.
.TP
.BI \-E " eofstr"
Set the logical end-of-file string; the first input item equal to
.I eofstr
ends input.  An empty
.I eofstr
disables this.  There is no default EOF string.
.TP
.BR \-e [\fIeofstr\fR]\fR, \fP \-\-eof [=\fIeofstr\fR]
Deprecated synonym for
.BR \-E .
A bare
.B \-e
disables the logical EOF string.
.TP
.BI \-I " replstr"
Insert mode: for each input line, replace every occurrence of
.I replstr
in the initial arguments with the line and invoke
.IR utility .
Implies
.B \-L 1
and
.BR \-x .
.B \-i
is a deprecated synonym defaulting
.I replstr
to
.BR {} .
.TP
.BI \-J " replstr"
Insert all collected items at the first argument containing
.IR replstr ,
honoring
.B \-n
and
.BR \-L .
.TP
.BI \-L " number"
Invoke
.I utility
for every
.I number
nonempty input lines.
.B \-l
is a deprecated synonym (default 1).
.TP
.BI \-n " number" "\fR, \fP" \-\-max\-args= number
Use at most
.I number
items per invocation.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-open\-tty
Reopen the child's standard input from
.B /dev/tty
so the invoked utility may be interactive.
.TP
.BI \-P " maxprocs" "\fR, \fP" \-\-max\-procs= maxprocs
Run up to
.I maxprocs
invocations concurrently.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-interactive
Prompt on
.B /dev/tty
before each invocation; only an affirmative reply runs the command.  Implies
.BR \-t .
.TP
.BI \-R " replcount"
With
.BR \-I ,
replace at most
.I replcount
occurrences of the replacement string (negative means unlimited).
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-no\-run\-if\-empty
Do not run the utility if there are no input items.
.TP
.BI \-S " replsize"
Set the size in bytes available for replacement-string expansion under
.B \-I
or
.BR \-J .
.TP
.BI \-s " size" "\fR, \fP" \-\-max\-chars= size
Limit each constructed command line to
.I size
bytes.  The default is derived from
.B ARG_MAX
less the current environment, floored at
.BR LINE_MAX .
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-verbose
Echo each command to the standard error before running it.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-exit
Exit if a command line would exceed the
.B \-s
size; implied by
.BR \-I .
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
All invocations succeeded.
.TP
.B 123
One or more invocations exited 1\-125.
.TP
.B 124
An invocation exited 255;
.B xargs
stopped.
.TP
.B 125
An invocation was killed by a signal;
.B xargs
stopped.
.TP
.B 126
.I utility
was found but could not be executed.
.TP
.B 127
.I utility
was not found.
.TP
.B 1
.B xargs
itself encountered an error (bad option, I/O, allocation).
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Remove every file listed in a manifest, NUL-safe:
.PP
.RS
.nf
find . \-name '*.tmp' \-print0 | xargs \-0 rm \-f
.fi
.RE
.PP
Copy each input path into a directory, one per invocation:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ls | xargs \-I{} cp {} /backup
.fi
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR echo (1),
.BR find (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR execvp (3)
.SH STANDARDS
The
.B xargs
utility conforms to IEEE Std 1003.1\-2024 (POSIX.1\-2024).  The
.BR \-0 ", " \-a ", " \-d ", " \-P ", " \-r ", " \-\-null
and related long options are GNU extensions; the
.BR \-J ", " \-o ", " \-R ", " \-S
options are BSD extensions.  Where the standards conflict, the BSD behavior
is provided.
